{"id":26632576291246,"type":"genre","name":"Monster funk: la tercera dinastía","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":16,"info":{"img":null},"own_scenes":[{"id":262,"name":"Monster funk: la tercera dinastía","book":{"id":4,"slug":"Funk_Vincent","title":"Funk: The Music, the People, and the Rhythm of the One","author":"Rickey Vincent","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/Funk/","families":{"raices":{"name":"Raíces: blues, jazz, gospel y R&B","color":"#d97706"},"soul":{"name":"Soul y proto-funk: Stax, Nueva Orleans, Motown","color":"#facc15"},"jb":{"name":"James Brown y los JB’s","color":"#ef4444"},"rock":{"name":"Funk psicodélico y black rock: Sly, Hendrix","color":"#c084fc"},"fusion":{"name":"Jazz-funk y fusión","color":"#38bdf8"},"bandas":{"name":"Bandas de funk de los setenta","color":"#22c55e"},"pfunk":{"name":"P-Funk: Parliament-Funkadelic","color":"#a3e635"},"disco":{"name":"Disco y dance funk","color":"#f472b6"},"ochenta":{"name":"Funk de los ochenta: Prince, Rick James, electro","color":"#22d3ee"},"hiphop":{"name":"Hip hop, go-go y funk de los noventa","color":"#94a3b8"},"cultura":{"name":"Política y cultura negra","color":"#e5e7eb"}}},"family":"Bandas de funk de los setenta","color":"#22c55e","start":1976,"end":1986,"place":"Dayton, Atlanta, Vallejo y Memphis","desc":"El fantasma del disco solo intensificó el funk de baile de los verdaderos funkers: la Edad de Oro del baile y la cima del movimiento. La influencia de P-Funk llenó las bandas de capas, viajes espaciales y personajes («Captain Quick Draw», «Big Chief Funkum», «Jupiter», «Wonder Worm» de Captain Sky, «Galaxy» de War, «Long Stroke» de ADC Band). Brick, quinteto de Atlanta, inventó con «Dazz» (1976) el «disco-jazz», con el bajo de Ray Ransom y la flauta funky de Jimmy Brown; nunca fue banda de disco. El Medio Oeste fue el semillero: Slave, de Dayton (Steve Washington, sobrino de un Ohio Player, el bajo de Mark Adams, la guitarra de Drac Hicks), con «Slide» y luego la voz de Steve Arrington; Sun («Radiation Level»); Lakeside, empaquetados por SOLAR con un disfraz por disco («It’s All the Way Live», «Fantastic Voyage»). De Vallejo, Con Funk Shun («Ffun»). «Con el funk no hay reglas», decía Washington.","aka":["Third Funk Dynasty","Slave","Brick","Lakeside","Con Funk Shun","Dayton funk"],"notes":[],"influences":[{"name":"Ohio Players","id":244121723056079,"type":"artist"},{"name":"La banda de funk","id":199585824693315,"type":"genre"},{"name":"Jazz-funk y fusión","id":224559931469406,"type":"genre"}],"refs":[{"ch":17,"q":"For those musicians capable and determined to throw down the very heavy funk, this was the Golden Age of Dance Music, and the high point of the funk movement overall.","para":"The specter of disco music only increased the intensity of dance funk produced by the new breed of true funkers. For those musicians capable and determined to throw down the very heavy funk, this was the Golden Age of Dance Music, and the high point of the funk movement overall. Despite the resonance of the disco beat, hard black funk bands thrived all over the country. From New York came Cameo; Dayton, Ohio, brought Slave, Sun, and Lakeside; Vallejo, California, spawned Con Funk Shun; well-traveled Bay Area jazz keyboardist George Duke scorched the black charts with some heavy fonk; the immortal Bar-Kays continued their Memphis soul tradition into the 1980s; and two youngsters of note got their start in the late seventies—Buffalo-bred Rick James, and Minneapolis’s own Prince Rogers Nelson.","page":273,"pdf":226,"chapter":"Cap. 16: Dance Funk: Do You Wanna Get Funky With Me?"},{"ch":17,"q":"The urban Midwest was the true hotbed of seventies funk.","para":"The urban Midwest was the true hotbed of seventies funk. Closely linked to their Dayton, Ohio, predecessors the Ohio Players, Slave grew out of the best of the local Dayton funk bands. Led by multitalented Stevie Washington, himself the nephew of Ohio Players sideman Ralph “Pee Wee” Middlebrooks, Slave was hooked up from the start. Grounded by the rock-hard bass thumps of Mark “Mr. Mark” Adams (who had the loudest bass thumps on black radio in the late seventies) and the atom-splitting thrashes of guitarist Mark “Drac” Hicks, there was a preposterous sense of strength coming from Slave. With bottom-heavy, jazzy, and often humorous flavors oozing through the band’s inexplicable yet ice-cold rhythm notions, Slave was a monster funk band in every sense of the phrase. “With the funk, there are no rules,” bandleader Stevie Washington explained. “You can put jazz with it … country with it, you can put classical with it, whatever the hell you feel. That’s what we all are saying.”","page":277,"pdf":229,"chapter":"Cap. 16: Dance Funk: Do You Wanna Get Funky With Me?"},{"ch":17,"q":"“Slide” was their first and biggest hit.","para":"“Slide” was their first and biggest hit. A thumping, sloppy dinosaur of a jam, it featured an encyclopedia of gimmicks, but when all was said and done, it was meat-and-potatoes bass and guitar that made it work. Ghoulish laughs, bicycle horns, goofy lines like “I’ll let my grandmother check that out” were all for fun, but the lumpy-hump bass, the scatological horn squeaks syncopated in drastic angular phrases, the wicked avalanche of groove at the change, and Drac’s rocket-blast of a guitar solo make “Slide” an all-time funk classic.","page":278,"pdf":230,"chapter":"Cap. 16: Dance Funk: Do You Wanna Get Funky With Me?"}],"artists":[{"id":235411483220935,"type":"artist","name":"Kool & The Gang","rating":null},{"id":195472159040058,"type":"artist","name":"The Commodores","rating":null},{"id":244121723056079,"type":"artist","name":"The Ohio Players","rating":null},{"id":2586454857410,"type":"artist","name":"The Undisputed Truth","rating":null},{"id":33943571657611,"type":"artist","name":"Coolio","rating":null},{"id":17653417132284,"type":"artist","name":"Slave","rating":null},{"id":119298292394476,"type":"artist","name":"Lakeside","rating":null},{"id":7608842085123,"type":